      "writtenOn": "2022-06-27T20:38:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is first time I make commits into kernel tree so not sure what\u0027s the best practice and would love to follow your advices. A couple reasons of squashing commits:\n1. When we originally took LKL from github project, we ingested the change as a big diff, so we *lost* the individual github LKL commits. I can surely recover that by redoing the merge, if that\u0027s preferred.\n\n2. Not including the individual github LKL commits, we already have 49 commits here after clean up -- I don\u0027t know if spamming kernel team with huge number of code reviews (\u003e100 if we consider using individual commits from LKL github project) is a good idea.\n\n3. Some of the files are changed back and forth (for example, we touched files outside of LKL subtree at the begining, and then reverted those changes as a cleanup effort). When squashing commits together, those files appear as not touched at all; but if we use individual commits, those will show up as changed by some individual commits -- Not sure if this will cause merge problems when we taking upstream linux mainline changes.\n\n4. Some fuzzers initially submitted with a large amount unnecessary test data which were later removed. There is no reason for them to show up in the history, so squashing changes will eliminate the traces of those files.\n\nAnyway, I\u0027m happy to take whatever recommendations from kernel team as long as it makes your work easy.",
      "parentUuid": "01261c9a_fc3aee03",
      "revId": "49c0f6edbe90fea47001515465b42662ddfbf6e1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
